**Verdict — Gemini’s shared-basis compensation inequality is correct after a repair. Its application to M3 and its general-pole inflation claim do not follow from the proofs given.**

- **Submodule KH — fixable.** An invertible ternary \(B\) preserves the \(k\)-span, but its generator ideals need not remain coprime to \(N\). Apply KH to the monomials in \(Bw\) inside the **original** ideal lattice, using the original unit-evaluation witness in the unchanged span. This gives \(Q\ge (x-D\log(C_kD))/T-\sum_i\log(M_{B,i}/t_i)\). One basis within \(e^{O(m)}\) of threshold on \(D-o(D)\) tags therefore suffices: Cramer charges the exceptions \(o(x/T)\). [Gemini’s application of KH](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_g/comp_proofs.txt:45) · [Repair](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/comp_cross/checks.txt:2)

- **M3 and M4 — asymptotic loss claims unproved.** In M3, \(\log b\asymp x/n\asymp n\log x\), so the exceptional count \(O(m+\log b)\) does **not** imply \(o(D)\). Also, \(B_0w=\pm\Delta^*\tilde v\) gives \(t_i\le M_{B_0,i}\). Near-optimal sharing requires the reverse bound \(M_{B_0,i}\le e^{O(m)}t_i\). In M4 the exhibited staircase amplification is \((1+\sqrt2)^{2m-1}\), whose logarithm is only \(\Theta(m)\). Unimodularity preserves the lattice and corresponding element norms, but it can send ternary coefficients outside the box. [M3 root count and threshold step](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_g/comp_proofs.txt:21) · [Box-transfer claim](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_g/comp_proofs.txt:66) · [M4 matrix](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_g/run_comp_adversary.py:174)

- **Two-place covolume and support width — correct within scope.** The determinant argument proves \(\tau_1\tau_2\ge(C_km)^{-2}\) for the **same** \(m\) independent integral vectors at both base places. Independently attained thresholds need not supply those vectors. The support-width minor correctly gives \(s_{\rm big}\ge(\gamma/e-o(1))m\). It counts large coordinates and supplies no lower bound at another embedding. [Covolume proof](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_g/comp_proofs.txt:74) · [Minor bound](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_g/comp_proofs.txt:88)

- **General-pole identity — correct; claimed dominance — wrong.** Take \(R=T(T-1)\), \(P=T^n+KT\), fixed \(n\ge4\), and even \(K\to\infty\). One root of \(P-\sqrt2\) approaches zero and the other \(n-1\) escape. At the small root, both Lagrange summands have magnitude \(\asymp K\), yet their sum is \(1+\alpha+\cdots+\alpha^{n-2}\to1\). This directly breaks the inference at line 113. [Claim](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_g/comp_proofs.txt:101) · [Counterexample](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/comp_cross/checks.txt:34)

- **Measurements — 55/55 zero recorded increments confirmed; unit count corrected.** My pre-registered exact replay of the cited 112-bit M2 box found **35 nonscalar capped orbits of rank four, of which three have norm one and span rank three**. These units defeat a universal nonscalar norm floor by itself. They do not refute the asymptotic disjunction. Gemini also filters on unit evaluation before computing thresholds, so its reported \(t_i\) upper-bound the full-box thresholds. [Replay](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/comp_cross/replay.txt:1) · [Threshold filter](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_g/run_comp_adversary.py:452) · [Parity counters](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_g/run_comp_adversary.py:642)

**The two open regimes are different.** Overlap concerns several small pole factors at one root. Sharing concerns whether one matrix has small total excess across roots. General modules need have no pole presentation. Gemini’s residual condition \(\max_B|U_B|=o(D)\) also misses the intermediate case of two large groups using different bases, neither covering almost all tags. [Claim under review](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/comp_gemini.out:100)

**Combining the proofs closes a larger class.** At the brief’s normalized quadratic-base balance, suppose \(Bw\) contains a scalar multiple of a rank-\(r\) split-pole tuple with \(r/m\to\theta>0\). If \(B\) is within \(e^{O(m)}\) of threshold except on \(fD\) tags, the split-pole **height estimate before subtracting Cramer**, followed by Gemini’s excess bound, gives
\[
Q(w)\ge(3\theta/2-f-o(1))\,mx/n.
\]
Thus \(f<3\theta/2\) with fixed margin suffices. For \(\theta=1\), even \(f=1\) works. This proves the threshold branch for admissible M3/M4 twists independently of their asserted loss frequencies. [Combined proof](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/comp_cross/checks.txt:13)

**Single remaining statement.** At the stated balance, prove that any remaining reduced linked family with \(H=O(x/T)\), loss \(\Omega(m\log m)\) on \(\Omega(D)\) tags, and **at least one eligible nonscalar row** with \(0<|F|/J\le Y\), \(\log Y\asymp\sqrt{x\log x}\), satisfies \(Q\ge c_kx/T\) for some \(c_k>0\). This is the brief’s compensation disjunction because the cap lies below the KH floor.